  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 441 AM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nThe forecast area is on the southern edge of the main westerlies \naloft, which cover the northern tier of the CONUS while the ridge \nhas been pushed just to our south. A convectively induced vort max \nnear the OK panhandle is on track to keep that batch of storms in \nsouthern KS this morning. Surface high pressure is now in control \nover the Central Plains, and the combination of light winds, clear \nskies, and cooling below crossover temperatures has allowed for some \npatchy fog to form. Soundings show this shallow fog should mix out \nan hour or so after sunrise, but in the meantime, be on the lookout \nfor quick reductions in visibility in low-lying areas. \n\nThe rest of today looks dry with partly cloudy skies and highs in \nthe 80s. One thing to watch towards the evening hours will be a \nshortwave ejecting out of the Northern Rockies into the High Plains \nthat is progged to spark some convection in western Nebraska. Given \nthe position of the LLJ, it would make sense that any convection \nthat sustains itself overnight would stay in Nebraska, which is what \nmost CAMs have shown. The 00z HRRR appears to be an outlier in \nbringing more scattered convection into our area overnight, but it's \nworth monitoring to ensure that is indeed the case. Most CAMs track \nthe storms through the MO River Valley overnight, which may end up \nclipping northern KS Tuesday morning mainly around the Hwy 36 \ncorridor.\n\nThe aforementioned convection Tuesday morning is the first factor \nthat complicates the forecast for the rest of Tuesday, which looks \nto be based on mesoscale processes difficult to ascertain at this \ntime range. If enough rain and cloud cover linger far enough south, \nthat could keep temperatures cooler than forecast and also have an \neffect on how much destabilization occurs. That convection could \nalso leave an outflow boundary that may or may not be the focus for \ndeveloping storms later. Interestingly, the 06z short-term guidance \nhas trended further south with the sfc low pressure in western KS, \nwhich keeps the warm front further south (consensus would have it \naround the KS River Valley). This then brings the cold front through \nthe area faster with storms potentially further south than previous \nruns had as well. Regardless of which side of the warm front you \nlook at, forecast soundings show a lot of CIN with a surface-based \nor mixed-layer parcel, so it would seem to be tough to get a storm \ngoing until the cold front comes through and triggers elevated \nstorms, which is what the CAMs seem to be showing. While deep-layer \nshear is sufficient for supercells, am skeptical as to how much of \nthat will be realized given that issue. Still, if a strong enough \nupdraft develops, can't rule out some storms producing damaging \nwinds or small hail. All this to say, there is decent confidence in \nstorms forming, but what is uncertain is the timing and intensity of \nthem. They should move out during the overnight hours.\n\nBy Wednesday, cooler temperatures are once again forecast behind the \nfront with highs back in the 80s. Northwest flow develops aloft with \na large trough encompassing eastern Canada into the Great Lakes \nregion. Another embedded perturbation aloft looks to trigger another \nround of thunderstorms Wednesday evening into early Thursday \nmorning, but uncertainty in the timing and placement of that wave \nkeeps PoPs limited to 30-50% for now. The rest of the week into next \nweekend sees the upper ridge building to our west, which favors at \nleast a slight warming trend. Cluster analysis shows good agreement \non that ridge, but it does show differing ideas on how well \namplified the Great Lakes trough will still be by the weekend. That \ncould have an effect on how much we will actually warm up if we keep \nmore easterly winds in the low levels. So while highs are forecast \nto be a bit warmer next weekend than the middle of this week, they \nare generally still close to average, and a small warmup seems \nreasonable at this time.",
